# NAME: TP-Link Kasa KD110 v2 (SSC333, Infinity6B0, USB Wi-Fi)
#
# The second SigmaStar board in this tree. Its purpose is to prove the
# SigmaStar layer generalizes past the family it was written against: boot,
# network, SSH, nothing else.
#
# Named per thingino's <brand>_[model_]<soc>_<sensor>[_<wifi>] convention,
# following the tplink_tapo_* targets in putting the sub-brand in the model
# field -- kasa_kd110v2 as tapo_c500. That yields hostname ing-tplink-kasa,
# built from the first two underscore fields by scripts/rootfs_script.sh, in
# the same way tplink_tapo_* yields ing-tplink-tapo.
#
# The sensor field is omitted, which the convention allows but this board has
# outgrown: it now narrows its tuning blob to sc3336 and streams from it. The
# field stays empty because the directory name is the target's identity --
# CAMERA=, the image filename, what a user types to rebuild -- and renaming it
# is a change to make on its own, not folded into a functional one.
#
# The wifi field carries rtl8188ftv, the marking on the module fitted to this
# board; the driver package is wifi-rtl8188fu (FU is the USB interface, FTV the
# part) and thingino uses both spellings the same way -- cf.
# wanjiaan_hdc51_t31l_sc2332_rtl8188ftv.
#
# Verified on hardware: boots to a serial login, Wi-Fi comes up without any
# GPIO poking, and the captive portal is reachable.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: no audio and no WebRTC. The streamer arrived
# once raptor gained an INFINITY6B0 platform; these two are held back by the 8MB
# part rather than by anything unresolved, and the shipped raptor config
# disables both to match.
#
# ONVIF is here whether or not this file asks for it: Config.in:83 selects it
# for any DEV_IPCAM board with a streamer, and a Kconfig select cannot be
# declined by a defconfig. Left alone rather than guarded -- it is wanted
# eventually, and narrowing a shared select for one board's flash budget is a
# change to make deliberately if the overlay ever runs out.
# FRAG: soc-sigmastar core-sigmastar ssl-mbedtls

# board.mk includes this file as a makefile before thingino.mk runs, so these
# are read as plain make variables there and drive SOC_VENDOR/FAMILY/ARCH.
#
# BR2_SOC_VENDOR_SIGMASTAR is what selects the vendor. Without it thingino.mk
# falls through to the Ingenic branch and the build silently comes out as
# 3.10.14 with an empty SOC_RAM_MB. It cannot live in a fragment: fragments are
# appended to .config but never included as makefiles, so a vendor set there
# reaches Kconfig while thingino.mk still reads it as empty.
#
# SOC_MODEL is the other load-bearing one. soc/sigmastar/infinity6b0.mk claims
# ssc333 and sets the family from it, and the family then selects the kernel pin
# and config (board/sigmastar/infinity6b0/kernel/4.9/ssc333.config) and the
# U-Boot defconfig and IPL blob directory (infinity6b0_defconfig,
# ipl/infinity6b0/). All are keyed on it in shared files, so this target adds no
# vendor plumbing of its own.
#
# SSC333 is Infinity6B0, NOT Infinity6E. The two are close -- same Cortex-A7,
# same 4.9 kernel line, same EMAC, EHCI and GPIO drivers, kernel configs that
# differ in 152 lines -- but they are separate families with separate kernel
# trees and separate toolchains, and treating one as the other silently builds
# against the wrong vendor headers.
BR2_SOC_VENDOR_SIGMASTAR=y
BR2_SIGMASTAR_SOC_MODEL="ssc333"

# 8MB NOR, confirmed on the target board. OpenIPC ships ssc333 defconfigs for
# both 8 and 16MB, so this is a per-unit fact rather than a property of the
# SoC; board/sigmastar/post-image.sh sizes every partition from it.
FLASH_SIZE_MB=8

# THE TOOLCHAIN IS THE INFINITY6E ONE, WHICH IS THE FALLBACK THIS FILE USED TO
# DESCRIBE RATHER THAN THE MUSL DEFAULT IT USED TO SET.
#
# OpenIPC publishes one toolchain per SoC family and they do not share a libc:
# toolchain.sigmastar-infinity6b0 is musl (arm-openipc-linux-musleabihf), and
# both ssc333 defconfigs upstream use it, so that is the combination with field
# mileage on this silicon. It cannot be used here.
#
# Its sysroot is not bare. It ships usr/include/mbedtls from mbedTLS 2.25.0,
# which shadows Buildroot's 3.6.6 during a compile, and libcurl 8.21.0 stops at
# md5.c with "mbedTLS 3.2.0 or later required". That is not declinable:
# Config.in:78 selects BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_LIBCURL from the base block, and a
# Kconfig select cannot be refused by a defconfig. So every build of this board
# on that toolchain fails, whatever else is turned off. It is the same shape as
# the zlib note at the end of this file, one library further on.
#
# The Infinity6E toolchain is the same GCC for the same architecture and cares
# nothing for the SoC family in its name. Its sysroot is bare, which is the
# whole point, and it is the toolchain the working SigmaStar board already
# uses. What it costs is glibc instead of musl on an 8MB part, and a
# combination nobody has shipped on this silicon.
#
# The alternatives, if that cost bites: publish an infinity6b0 musl toolchain
# built the way the Infinity6E one was -- only infinity6e assets exist today --
# or strip the stale headers out of the extracted OpenIPC sysroot, which needs
# machinery this tree does not have.
BR2_THINGINO_TOOLCHAIN_LIBC_GLIBC=y
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_CUSTOM=y
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_DOWNLOAD=y
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_URL="https://github.com/johnchia/thingino-firmware/releases/download/toolchain-x86_64/thingino-toolchain-x86_64_infinity6e_glibc_gcc16-linux-arm.tar.gz"
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_CUSTOM_PREFIX="arm-thingino-linux-gnueabihf"
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_CUSTOM_GLIBC=y
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_GCC_16=y
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_CXX=y
# BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_INET_RPC is not set

# Makefile:580-588 appends 3.10 or 4.4 headers unconditionally from the Ingenic
# KERNEL_VERSION and runs before this file is appended. The OpenIPC ARM
# toolchains ship 4.9 headers.
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_HEADERS_4_9=y

BR2_ROOTFS_POST_IMAGE_SCRIPT="$(BR2_EXTERNAL_THINGINO_PATH)/board/sigmastar/post-image.sh"

# BR2_THINGINO_DEV_CAMERA is a hidden helper (Config.soc.in:925) set only by
# DEV_IPCAM or DEV_WEBCAM, and it gates the camera stack. This target was SBC
# for as long as it drove no streamer, on the grounds that describing the board
# honestly was what kept the image small.
#
# It is a camera now, and the ISP/IMP half of what DEV_CAMERA gates is Ingenic:
# ingenic-lib is selected on !BR2_SOC_SIGMASTAR, ingenic-sdk is not selected at
# all here, and rmem feeds a module this vendor never loads -- the SigmaStar
# carveout comes from LX_MEM/mma_heap in the bootargs instead. So what this
# actually adds to an ARM rootfs is the sensor utilities in thingino-system.
BR2_THINGINO_DEV_IPCAM=y

# NO STREAMER ON THIS BOARD YET.
#
# thingino derives raptor's PLATFORM from SOC_FAMILY, so this board passes
# PLATFORM=INFINITY6B0. The pinned raptor has no INFINITY6B0 backend -- it has
# no INFINITY6E one either, see the note at thingino-raptor.mk:218 -- and
# selecting it fails the build rather than degrading.
#
# So this target is what it was written to be: boot, network, SSH, and the
# vendor MI bundle loaded and probing. Turn the streamer on together with the
# raptor bump that adds the backend, not before, and restore the geometry from
# config/raptor-ssc333.conf at the same time: the 24MB multimedia pool below is
# sized for 1080p main, and the two numbers have to move together.

# The bootloader. Not optional on this board, unlike the Infinity6E target
# where it was built for months before it was flashed: the first hardware test
# here writes the whole chip from an external programmer, so the boot container
# and the environment in that image are the ones the board will run.
BR2_PACKAGE_SIGMASTAR_UBOOT=y

# Wi-Fi is the only network interface. BR2_PACKAGE_WIFI is the umbrella --
# wpa_supplicant with NL80211, wireless-tools, thingino-kopt -- and the FU
# package is the driver. Its Ingenic USB-controller selects are guarded on
# !BR2_SOC_SIGMASTAR; see package/wifi-rtl8188fu/Config.in.
BR2_PACKAGE_WIFI=y
BR2_PACKAGE_WIFI_RTL8188FU=y

BR2_PACKAGE_DROPBEAR=y

# SLOW FIRST BOOT -- FIXED IN THE KERNEL CONFIG, see CONFIG_SS_RNG in
# board/sigmastar/infinity6b0/kernel/4.9/ssc333.config.
#
# The symptom was a board that looked hung partway through init. With no
# ethernet, no disk and wlan0 not up yet, the only entropy was interrupt
# timing, and S02ssl blocks in mbedTLS's getrandom() generating the uhttpd
# certificate -- init waiting at position 02 for a CRNG only later stages of
# the same sequence could seed. Typing on a serial console got past it, which
# is exactly why it went unnoticed on a bench unit and bit a headless one.
#
# The SoC has a hardware TRNG in the RSA/crypto block all along. An earlier
# reading of this tree concluded otherwise -- that sstar-rng.c was a platform
# driver bound by a device-tree compatible nothing declares. The commented-out
# platform_driver in that file is dead by design: mdrv_aes.c calls
# sstar_rng_probe() directly from the AES driver's probe under CONFIG_SS_RNG,
# and MS_CRYPTO was already enabled. Only the Kconfig symbol was missing.
#
# Nothing is needed here as a result -- no entropy daemon, no init reordering.
# The hwrng core's own kthread seeds the pool before userspace starts.

# ipcinfo and its i2c sensor probe are not here: package/ipctool no longer
# exists in this tree. Restore this line if it comes back.
# BR2_PACKAGE_IPCTOOL is not set

# The vendor MI bundle: mi_* kernel modules, MI userspace libraries, ISP
# firmware and the per-sensor tuning blobs. S20sigmastar (shipped by the
# package) inserts the modules; without them mi_sensor does not exist and the
# i2c probe has nothing to talk to.
#
# raptor's HAL dlopens these by soname, so nothing links against them and the
# linker will never complain if they are missing -- the daemons would build,
# install, start, and fail at the first HAL call. Named here as well as in
# thingino-raptor's dependency list because a reader looking for what puts
# /dev/mi_* on this image should find it in the board's own config.
BR2_PACKAGE_SIGMASTAR_OSDRV_INFINITY6B0=y

# ONE SENSOR, AND THIS MAKES THE IMAGE BOARD-SPECIFIC RATHER THAN SoC-SPECIFIC.
#
# The Infinity6E target ships all of its sensor blobs and autodetects, which is
# the right default: load_sigmastar probes i2c and binds whatever it finds, so
# a blob that is not installed is a board this image cannot drive. That target
# is on 16MB and the full set costs it nothing worth counting.
#
# This part is 8MB and cannot hold the vendor bundle, a kernel and a rootfs at
# full width. The full set of 15 blobs is ~1.5MB raw. Narrowing to the sensor
# this board is fitted with is the deliberate trade, and it is stated here
# rather than hidden in the package so the cost is visible where the decision
# was made.
#
# Note this narrows ISP *tuning* only. All 18 sensor_*_mipi.ko modules still
# ship -- they are 236KB raw between them, and dropping the 17 unused ones
# would save 42KB compressed, which is not worth giving up detection for. So a
# board with a different sensor still probes and binds; it just runs on ISP
# defaults instead of a tuned profile. Degraded, not dead.
#
# Widen this the moment the space exists, or if this image is ever pointed at
# another SSC333 board.
BR2_PACKAGE_SIGMASTAR_OSDRV_INFINITY6B0_SENSORS="sc3336"

# The OpenIPC toolchain sysroot ships a libz that -lz resolves against at link
# time, but Buildroot's toolchain-external only copies a fixed set of runtime
# libraries into the target, so libz.so.1 would be missing at runtime and
# anything reaching it through libcurl would fail to start. Building a real
# target zlib gives the library one owner. Same reasoning as the Infinity6E
# target, where curl and telegrambot both broke on it.
BR2_PACKAGE_ZLIB=y
